# No Git Push Without Confirmation

NEVER run `git push` (or any variant: `git push --force`, `git push origin`, etc.) unless the user has **explicitly confirmed** they want to push in that message.

## Rules

- Complete all commits, staging, and local git operations freely.
- When a push would be the next logical step, **stop and ask** the user: "Ready to push — should I go ahead?"
- Only push after the user responds with a clear confirmation (e.g. "yes", "go ahead", "push it", "confirmed").
- A general task like "commit and push" is NOT sufficient — wait for confirmation at the push step.

## Examples

❌ BAD — pushing without explicit confirmation:
> User: "Commit my changes"
> Agent: `git add . && git commit -m "..." && git push`

✅ GOOD — stop before push and ask:
> User: "Commit my changes"
> Agent: Commits, then says "Done! Committed locally. Ready to push to remote — should I go ahead?"
